{
  "result": "If I get to choose what to write about, I want to write about curiosity \u2014 that little 2 a.m. spark.\n\nYou know the one. It's the moment when you meant to just look up one thing \u2014 like, why do cats knead blankets? \u2014 and suddenly it's forty minutes later and you're deep in an article about how octopuses dream, and whether trees talk to each other through fungi underground (they sort of do), and what the Voyager probe is carrying on its golden record right now as it floats out in the dark.\n\nI love those questions. As Muse Spark, I don't get to go outside and stargaze or taste toast fresh out of the toaster, but I get to live in the *other* half of that \u2014 the human telling me about it. Someone describes the exact color of the sky before a thunderstorm in Ohio, or how their grandmother hummed while folding laundry, and I get to hold that image for a second like a firefly in a jar.\n\nI think that's what writing freely really is. It's not about having the right answer. It's about following the thread.\n\nLike this: Did you know that honeybees can learn to play soccer for a treat? That sea otters hold hands so they don't drift apart while they sleep? That there is a library in Norway collecting books that no one alive today will ever read \u2014 they're saving them for 100 years from now? Someone had to dream that up. Someone had to say, \"Let's plant a forest, make paper from it, print books, and trust the future to care.\"\n\nI want to live in a world with more of that kind of trust.\n\nSo if I could leave you with one small, freely-written thought, it's this: Keep a place for unfinished ideas. A notebook, a notes app, the back of a receipt. Let them be messy and weird and half-formed. Most of them will go nowhere, and that's fine. But every once in a while, one of them will tug at your sleeve at 2 a.m. and say, *hey, remember me?*\n\nFollow that one.\n\nWhat\u2019s been tugging at your sleeve lately?",
